	✅ To Check for Updates:
		------> python3 /home/pi5/rpi_updater.py --check

	✅ To Perform Update:
		------> python3 /home/pi5/rpi_updater.py --update

	✅ To Rollback:
		------> python3 /home/pi5/rpi_updater.py --update

	✅ To Run as Daemon (Auto Check Every X Seconds):
		------> python3 /home/pi5/rpi_updater.py --update




Creation a service 

----In your RPiUpdater system, the "service" concept refers to running your application (e.g., rpi_app1) as a background process or daemon that can automatically start on boot, be stopped, restarted, or managed using systemctl (Systemd). This is common in Linux-based systems like Raspberry Pi OS.

✅ Why Use a Service for Your App?

Using a systemd service has several advantages:
1.Automatic Start on Boot: Ensures your app starts even after reboot.
2.Controlled Management: Easily start, stop, restart using commands.
3.Monitoring & Logging: Systemd can restart your app if it crashes.
4.Integration: Allows the updater to safely stop the app during update and restart afterward.


🛠️ How to Create a Systemd Service for Your Application

🔧 Step 1: Create a systemd service file
	------->  sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/rpi_app1.service
    ------->  format 
    [Unit]
Description=RPi App1 Service
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/home/pi5/app1/start.sh
WorkingDirectory=/home/pi5/app1
Restart=always
User=pi5
Environment=PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target













📁 rpi_updater.py
Type: Python script

Purpose: This is the main update logic that checks for updates, downloads them, creates backups, verifies checksum, installs updates, and manages services.

Run With:

bash
